+## Testing without a simulator
+
+`scripts/cvfrmap-fake.py` (in the NavigationApp repo, a copy of the cvfr-bridge fake) serves the
+bridge's JSON on port 2020 with a synthetic aircraft, so the connection and the in-flight features
+can be tried without X-Plane. Standard library Python only:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/cvfrmap-fake.py                       # flies scripts/routes/LLHZ-to-LLHA.json, looping
+python3 scripts/cvfrmap-fake.py --route my-route.json # an exported NavAid route
+python3 scripts/cvfrmap-fake.py --figure-eight        # rate-1 figure-8 over LLBG
+python3 scripts/cvfrmap-fake.py --speed-factor 10 --wind-dir 270 --wind-speed 20
+```
+
+Then connect to `http://localhost:2020` (or the computer's LAN address from the phone app).
+`--reverse` flies the route back again; a crosswind really drifts the aircraft, for the drift alert.
+
 ## Compatible bridges
 
 - **[Little NavMap](https://albar965.github.io/littlenavmap.html)** (MSFS 2020/2024, X-Plane, P3D, FSX) — recommended
